        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Running Costs Over a Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price is only part of the cost. Manual back shavers use replacement cartridges or blades that typically last several shaves each, depending on hair density and how well you rinse and dry them. At the time of writing, replacement blades generally cost a few dollars apiece. Shaving every week, that adds up to roughly $30 to $80 a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric shavers have fewer ongoing costs but larger ones. Replacement foils and cutter heads often cost $15 to $40 and last months to a year or more, depending on use. Batteries eventually lose capacity after a few years; check whether the brand sells replacement batteries or whether a weak battery means a new tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check how easy it is to buy the replacements before you buy the handle. Brands with their own proprietary blades can discontinue them, leaving a perfectly good handle useless. Shavers that accept widely sold blade formats, or brands that have kept the same cartridge for years, are a safer bet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bundled extra blades are worth it if the per-blade price is lower than buying separately. Avoid bundles padded with low-value extras such as cheap gel or travel pouches. Divide the bundle price by the number of shaves you will realistically get from the blades to see the real cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cleaning and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rinse a manual shaver under warm running water after every few strokes and thoroughly when you finish. Shake it out and let it air-dry head up, out of the shower if possible. Water left on the blade dulls the edge and can rust it. Wiping the blade gently with a towel can damage the edge, so dab or air-dry instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean electric shavers according to the manual. Rinse waterproof models under the tap, then let them dry with the foil or head removed. Brush out hair from non-waterproof models with the included brush. A drop of clipper oil on trimmer blades every few uses keeps them cutting smoothly and running cooler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not share a back shaver. Blades can carry bacteria and blood-borne germs, even when the skin looks fine. Disinfect blades occasionally with rubbing alcohol, especially after a nick, and replace blades that pull or feel rough rather than pushing through one more shave.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the shaver in a dry spot with the blade guard on. Charge lithium-ion electrics periodically if you only use them every few weeks, and avoid leaving them in a hot car or a steamy bathroom for long periods, which shortens battery life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a razor through long hair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A razor on long back hair clogs, tugs and skips, which is when it nicks. Trim to stubble with an electric groomer or a trimmer attachment first, then shave. The extra two minutes of trimming saves irritation and a lot of rinsing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing hard because you cannot see</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without visual feedback, most men push the blade too hard, causing razor burn and cuts. Use light, steady strokes and let the tool's weight do the work. Check the result with two mirrors or a phone camera instead of making extra hard passes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaving against the grain on the first pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Going against the grain lifts hairs and cuts them below the skin line, a common cause of ingrown hairs on the back. Shave with the grain first. Only try a second pass across or against the grain if your skin stays calm after several shaves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a dull blade too long</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A worn blade drags and pulls rather than cutting cleanly, which irritates skin and leaves patches. Replace cartridges at the first sign of tugging. Rinse and air-dry the blade after each use to keep the edge longer, and never wipe it with a towel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaving right before a beach day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh shaving can leave redness or small bumps that take a day to fade, and salt water and sunscreen sting. Shave the evening before or two days ahead, moisturize, and let the skin settle before sun and swimming.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

