        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which back shaver should a first-timer buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your back hair is thick or your skin is sensitive, start with an electric model like the Mangroomer Ultimate Pro, which trims safely without a razor touching your skin. If you want smooth skin and your hair is light, a manual shaver such as the BAKblade 2.0 ELITE PLUS or the Bearback gives a closer result once you learn the angle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will back hair come back thicker after shaving?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Shaving cuts the hair at the surface and does not affect the follicle. Regrowth feels coarser because the cut end is blunt rather than tapered, and it may look darker before it is exposed to sun. The number of hairs, their thickness and their growth rate stay the same.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I really do my whole back without help?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with the right tool. A long handle, 18 inches or more, and a head that sits flat at the right angle let most men reach every area, including between the shoulder blades. Use two mirrors or a phone camera to check for missed patches. Men with limited shoulder mobility should pick a handle with an adjustable head.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I shave or trim my back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a smooth look, shave every two to four days, since stubble appears quickly. For a short trimmed look with an electric shaver, every one to two weeks is usually enough. Many men trim regularly and only shave fully before beach trips or events, which keeps irritation and blade costs down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop razor bumps on my back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Trim before shaving, shower first to soften hair, use a gel, shave with the grain using light pressure, and replace dull blades. Exfoliate gently a day or two before shaving and moisturize afterward. If bumps persist, switch to an electric trimmer, which leaves a short stubble and rarely causes ingrown hairs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a body groomer as good as a dedicated back shaver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A body groomer like the Philips Norelco Bodygroom 7000 works on the back only if the kit includes a long back attachment, and that attachment is usually less rigid than a dedicated handle. It is a good choice if you also groom your chest, arms and legs. If the back is your only target, a dedicated back shaver reaches more easily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is laser worth it instead of shaving?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Laser can reduce back hair long-term and suits men with dark hair on lighter skin who are tired of maintenance. It takes several sessions, costs hundreds to thousands of dollars, and may need touch-ups. For most men, a back shaver is far cheaper and more flexible. Some use both, shaving between laser sessions as the clinic directs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

